 "substitutedBy": null,
 "summary": "Relates to horsemen's health, welfare, pension and administrative benefits for harness racing licensees in the absence of contractual obligations; requires that in the absence of a contract between the licensed harness racing corporation or association and the representative horsemen's organization, the state gaming commission shall as a condition of racing require an association or corporation to withhold and pay eight percent of all unpaid and existing monies and to pay such sum to the horsemen's organization quarterly at facilities located in Westchester and Nassau county.",
 "title": "Relates to horsemen's health, welfare, pension and administrative benefits for harness racing licensees in the absence of contractual obligations",
 "vetoMessages": {
  "items": [
   {
    "billId": {
     "basePrintNo": "A3475",
     "basePrintNoStr": "A3475-2023",
     "printNo": "A3475",
     "session": 2023,
     "version": ""
    },
    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": " \n                          VETO MESSAGE - No. 23\n \nTO THE ASSEMBLY:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nAssembly Bill Number 3475-A, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT to amend the racing, pari-mutuel wagering and breeding law,\n      in relation to horsemen's health, welfare, pension and administra-\n      tive benefits for harness  racing  licensees  in  the  absence  of\n      contractual obligations\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  This  bill  would  require  the  Gaming Commission to distribute eight\npercent of purse money to a horsemen's organization in  the  event  that\nthe horseman and horse racing corporation could not successfully negoti-\nate a contract.\n \n  The  purse is a sum of money distributed by a racetrack to the winners\nof each race. The law currently allows a  horse  racing  corporation  or\nassociation to enter into a contractual agreement with the horsemen that\nwould  allow the distribution of a portion of the purse money to be used\nby the horsemen's organization to pay  for  certain  expenses,  such  as\nhealth care premiums.\n \n  A horse racing corporation or association has an interest in maintain-\ning  higher  purses.  These  higher  purses  allow  larger  prizes to be\nawarded, which in turn attracts  more  participants  and  increases  the\ninterest  in  the races at their facilities. The horsemen's organization\nhas an interest in using purse money to pay for some of their  organiza-\ntion's  expense  prior  to  that  purse  money  being distributed to the\nwinners. The law currently allows these two sides to negotiate an agreed\nupon amount to be used by the horsemen's organization.\n \n  This bill would impede those contractual  negotiations.  Specifically,\nthis  bill  would  require  the  Gaming  Commission  to distribute eight\npercent of purse money to the horsemen's organization if the  two  sides\nhad  not  reached a negotiated contract. This is a benefit that needs to\nbe bargained for between the parties.  Absent  a  strong  public  policy\nreason  for  doing  so,  the State should not interfere with contractual\nnegotiations through the legislative process.\n \n  Therefore, I am constrained to veto this bill.\n \n  The bill is disapproved.                       (signed) KATHY HOCHUL\n \n                               __________\n",
    "signedDate": null,
    "signer": "KATHY HOCHUL",
    "vetoNumber": 23,
    "vetoType": "STANDARD",
    "year": 2024
   }
  ],
